                "text": "**Analyzing the Transformation**\n\nOkay, I'm trying to figure out this transformation. The input has single-color pixels. The output stretches them horizontally and vertically, right? Example 1 seems key: (1,2) becomes 2, (2,6) to 7, and (5,3) gives me... something else. The horizontal/vertical extension might be the key?\n\n\n**Determining the Direction**\n\nOkay, the pattern is clearer now. Each input's output expands outwards, but the direction depends on its position relative to the center of the grid. It's a quadrant-based expansion. Top-left goes up and left; top-right, up and right; bottom-left, down and left; bottom-right, down and right. I'm going to test this with Example 2.\n\n\n**Refining the Logic**\n\nOkay, the quadrant theory needs tweaking. My focus now is on how the pixel expands. It seems the direction depends on comparing the row and column indices to the center of the grid, but there's a definite pattern. It seems row indices <= center expand UP. Column indices <= center expand LEFT. I have updated my approach to align with the new logic, with a focus on edge cases to fully understand.\n\n\n**Confirming the Expansion**\n\nI've tested the expansion behavior further, especially focusing on how pixels spread out across the grid. Now, I'm confident that if the row index is less than half the height, it expands upwards. If the column index is less than half the width, the expansion is leftwards. These rules seem to handle the examples correctly, even with odd dimensions.\n\n\n",
